# Personal Coding Preferences

## Minimize scope

Use the simplest correct diff. Do not add or change unrelated code, especially for question-only or review-only tasks. A focused 5-line fix beats a 100-line refactor.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — unrelated cleanup while fixing a null check
function getUser(id: string) {
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === id);
  if (!user) return null;
  return { ...user, displayName: formatName(user) }; // new helper, out of scope
}

// ✅ GOOD — fix only what was asked
function getUser(id: string) {
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === id);
  return user ?? null;
}
```

## Avoid over-engineering

Do not abstract one-off logic into helpers. Do not add excessive error handling for impossible or extremely unlikely edge cases.

## Use existing conventions

Read surrounding code before writing. Match naming, types, abstractions, import style, and documentation level. Reuse and extend existing functions rather than reimplementing similar logic.

## Comments

Good code should mostly be self-explanatory. Add comments only for non-obvious business logic or deep technical details.

## Testing

### Tests

Add tests only when requested or when they meaningfully cover real behavior. Do not add tests that trivially assert the obvious.

### TDD

Changing production code should come with related changes to tests, whenever possible.
The tests can be written first, to validate the behavior. And then the production code is changed or added to support the tests.
Tests and the code under test should be committed together - we should not have commits that are only test files.

### Types of Tests

Prefer integration style when possible - use real components and infrastructure if it is easy and cheap to do scope
Otherwise, use stubs, mocks, and fakes to handle cases when we cannot use real components